  "summary": "These passages bring several hopes for a God-appointed ruler into conversation. Second Samuel 7 promises David an enduring house; Psalm 2 portrays the LORD’s anointed king; Psalm 110 joins royal enthronement with priestly language; and Isaiah and Micah picture a ruler associated with justice, peace, and David’s line. Isaiah 52–53 adds the servant’s suffering and exaltation. The New Testament explicitly reads Jesus’ suffering, resurrection, and exaltation in light of these Scriptures. The images belong to different passages and settings, so the theme is a developing biblical pattern rather than one description repeated unchanged.",
  "historical_context": "The anchors span Israel’s royal promise, psalms about kingship, prophetic hopes, and later Christian interpretation. Second Samuel 7 frames the Davidic promise as a word to David about his house and throne. Psalms 2 and 110 use royal and priestly imagery; Isaiah 9 and 11 portray a ruler marked by justice, while Isaiah 52–53 presents the servant’s suffering and vindication. Micah 5 locates its ruler in Bethlehem. Luke 24 and Acts 2 show early Christian readers interpreting Israel’s Scriptures in relation to Jesus. Those New Testament readings should be distinguished from what each earlier passage says in its own literary setting.",
