  "literary_context": "The theme moves through different forms: a dynastic promise in 2 Samuel 7, royal and priestly psalms in Psalms 2 and 110, prophetic royal poems in Isaiah 9 and 11, the servant poems in Isaiah 52–53, and Micah’s oracle about a ruler from Bethlehem. The New Testament then interprets these Scriptures in narratives, sermons, and letters: Luke 24 presents Jesus explaining the Scriptures, Acts 2 proclaims him as Lord and Messiah, and Philippians 2 describes his humiliation and exaltation. This range helps explain why messianic hope includes more than one image of leadership.",
  "covenantal_significance": "The Davidic promise in 2 Samuel 7 supplies one important strand: hope for a lasting royal house. The psalms and prophets develop images of an anointed ruler, just governance, priestly service, and a servant who suffers. The New Testament presents Jesus’ suffering and exaltation as the way these hopes converge, while citing different passages for different parts of that account. Keeping the strands distinct prevents a reader from assuming that every earlier text makes the same direct prediction or describes the Messiah in the same terms.",
